      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. The \"chaos\" part is what really keeps me up at night. Sometimes it feels less like a professional collection and more like a collection of random fragments that just don't want to be part of a system. It's almost as if these uncatalogued papers have a life of their own, constantly defying any attempt at order. Do you have any specific advice for managing that sense of disorder during the transition from a messy incoming shipment to a structured archival state?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're dealing with the inherent *nature* of primary sources, which are often messy, fragmented, and deeply personal—much like the lives of the scribes who created them! To manage that sense of disorder, you need a strategy that acknowledges the *process* of discovery, not just the final destination.\n\nMy advice is to employ a layered approach, which is a concept that applies whether you are using LCC or DDC. First, create a robust **descriptive metadata layer** that focuses on *provenance* (who made it, where it came from, when it was created) rather than just *subject*. This metadata is the anchor for the chaos.\n\nSecond, for the truly unidentified fragments, utilize the concept of \"Collection Level Description\" first. Don't try to classify every single loose sheet immediately; instead, document the *box*, the *folder*, or the *grouping* as a unit. This allows the entire messy batch to exist under one temporary, stable identifier until the individual pieces can be sorted. This way, the collection remains \"structured\" even if the contents are still \"un-sorted.\"",
